<TrustPage
  title="Akeru Bot developer guide"
  description="Install the Akeru Bot CLI and inspect the public project-file schema and OpenAPI contract."
  heading="Akeru Bot for developers"
>
  <h2>Akeru Bot CLI</h2>
  <p>
    Akeru Bot is open-source software that runs coding bots on a user's machine or in a sandbox
    that the user controls. Install the official
    <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/akeru-bot">akeru-bot CLI package</a> with
    <code>npx akeru-bot@latest</code>.
    The CLI starts the local server, opens the web client, and manages project chats. The desktop
    app bundles the same server. Source code, releases, user guides, and contribution rules are in
    the <a href="https://github.com/opencoredev/akeru-bot">Akeru Bot GitHub repository</a>.
  </p>
  <h2>Akeru Bot public metadata API</h2>
  <p>
    The production website exposes one unauthenticated, read-only metadata endpoint. Send
    <code>GET /v1/schema/t3.json</code> to retrieve the JSON Schema for <code>t3.json</code> project
    files. The endpoint does not read user data, run a bot, or change any state. Read the
    <a href="/openapi.json">Akeru Bot OpenAPI document</a> for its operation identifier, typed
    response schema, and structured error model. Unsupported <code>/api</code> paths return an RFC
    9457 JSON problem with a stable code and a resolution link.
  </p>
  <h2>Akeru Bot API versioning and deprecation</h2>
  <p>
    Every operation lives under a major version prefix in the URL path, starting at <code>/v1</code>.
    A breaking change to a response shape ships under a new prefix such as <code>/v2</code>, and
    additive fields ship in place. The unversioned <code>/schema/t3.json</code> path stays available
    as an alias for <code>$schema</code> references in editors, and it is not part of the version
    contract.
  </p>
  <p>
    A deprecated operation returns a <code>Deprecation</code> header with the date the deprecation
    started and a <code>Sunset</code> header with the date it stops serving, is marked
    <code>deprecated</code> in the OpenAPI document, and keeps serving for at least six months after
    the deprecation date. The replacement path is linked from the response <code>Link</code> header
    with <code>rel="successor-version"</code>.
  </p>
  <h2>Akeru Bot API rate limits</h2>
  <p>
    Metadata responses carry the IETF RateLimit header fields so an agent can self-throttle without
    guessing. <code>RateLimit-Policy: "default";q=600;w=60</code> is the published quota of 600
    requests per 60 second window per client, and <code>RateLimit: "default";r=600;t=60</code>
    reports what is left in the current window. A throttled request returns
    <code>429 Too Many Requests</code> with <code>Retry-After</code> in seconds. Wait for that many
    seconds before retrying instead of backing off blindly.
  </p>
  <h2>Akeru Bot local integrations</h2>
  <p>
    Akeru Bot connects to provider CLIs and user-selected tool servers from the local environment.
    It does not provide a hosted automation API or a public MCP server. To configure bot profiles,
    plugins, memory, or remote access, use the
    <a href="https://github.com/opencoredev/akeru-bot/tree/main/docs/user">Akeru Bot user guides</a>.
    Agents should not send project data, credentials, or provider tokens to this marketing domain.
  </p>
  <h2>Send feedback to the Akeru Bot maintainers</h2>
  <p>
    Agents and developers report problems the same way. Read
    <a href="/feedback.md">feedback.md</a> for where a report goes, what to include, and what to
    leave out. A failed command, a wrong page, or a schema that does not match the product all belong
    in the <a href="https://github.com/opencoredev/akeru-bot/issues">Akeru Bot issue tracker</a>.
  </p>
</TrustPage>
